        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 901,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 691,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes the natural log of (1 + x) for every element of a tensor - kept\naccurate when x is tiny.\n\nIt turns small fractional changes (a 0.1% gain, a tiny probability) into\nlogs without losing them: log(1 + 0.00000001) computed by adding first\ngives 0 in 32-bit floats, this gives 0.00000001.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0, rising ever more slowly to the right (1.79 at 5), plunging\ntowards minus infinity as x approaches -1. Near 0 it is almost exactly x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.log1p\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n-1 gives minus infinity; anything below -1 gives NaN (not a number). The\ndemo starts at -0.9 for that reason.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-0.9 to 5 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log(1 + x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -0.9 to 5,\ndrawn from -2.5 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch (keep start above -1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.expm1 is its inverse, with the same care near zero.\nt.special.xlog1py multiplies the result by a second value, safely when\nthat value is 0.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 675,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes the natural log of (1 + x) for every element of a tensor - kept\naccurate when x is tiny.\n\nIt turns small fractional changes (a 0.1% gain, a tiny probability) into\nlogs without losing them: log(1 + 0.00000001) computed by adding first\ngives 0 in 32-bit floats, this gives 0.00000001.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0, rising ever more slowly to the right (1.79 at 5), plunging\ntowards minus infinity as x approaches -1. Near 0 it is almost exactly x.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.log1p\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n-1 gives minus infinity; anything below -1 gives NaN (not a number). The\ndemo starts at -0.9 for that reason.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-0.9 to 5 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log(1 + x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -0.9 to 5,\ndrawn from -2.5 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch (keep start above -1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.expm1 is its inverse, with the same care near zero.\nt.special.xlog1py multiplies the result by a second value, safely when\nthat value is 0."
        },
